  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T06:10:44.655Z",
  "answer": "### Issue Presented\nIs an employee who earned purely compensation income from one employer for the entire year, with correct tax withheld and remitted, required to file an income tax return?\n\n### Short Answer\nNo, the employee is not required to file an income tax return. Under Section 51 of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC), individuals whose sole income is purely compensation income, and for whom the income tax has been correctly withheld, are exempt from filing.\n\n### Accounting Treatment\nFor accounting purposes, the employer should ensure that the correct amount of tax is withheld from the employee's compensation income throughout the year. This withholding should be reported and remitted to the Bureau of Internal Revenue (BIR) as required.\n\n### Tax Treatment\nAccording to Section 51 of the NIRC, an individual whose taxable income does not exceed P250,000 and whose income tax has been correctly withheld is not required to file an income tax return. Since the employee's income is purely compensation and tax has been withheld, they fall under this exemption.\n\n### Differences and Reconciliation\nThe key difference lies in the requirement to file an income tax return. While employees earning income from multiple employers or those whose income exceeds the threshold must file, those with a single employer and correct withholding do not have this obligation. This reconciliation aligns with the provisions of the NIRC, ensuring compliance without unnecessary filing.\n\n### Practical Note\nIt is advisable for the employee to retain documentation of their income and the withholding tax remittance for future reference, even if they are not required to file. This ensures they have proof of compliance should any inquiries arise from the BIR.",
